For the Death intensity I decided to use the formula which is the ((Average Annual tornado + Flood+ Hurricane Deaths)/ average U.S. population) * 1000000. With this the Census Bureau provides the information needed to estimate the population and death intensity. 
When adjusting for the storm deaths based on population it shows a stronger decline than what the raw data shows. The raw data shows that the annual average fell from about 262.3 deaths in the 1940s to around 195.1 deaths in 2010 which is a decline of about 26%. However looking at the population of the U.S. it shows that over that same time period that the population doubled. In my calculation of the death intensity it shows that the death rate significantly fell to .61 deaths per million in the 2010’s which is a stark contrast from its 1.88 deaths per million in the 1940’s, which in total was about a 67% drop. This shows that the U.S. population's risk of dying in a tornado, hurricane, or flood has become lower over time and the population adjusted data shows a significant improvement in death rates as compared to the fatalities alone.    

